# Local dashboard configuration — copy to `.env.local` and fill in.
#
#   cp .env.local.example .env.local
#
# `next dev` and `next build` read `.env.local`; `.gitignore` keeps it out of the
# repo. This example file is the one `.env*` exception.
#
# WHAT THIS IS NOT: credentials. Every value below is a resource name, URL or
# ARN — configuration, not a secret. The dashboard signs its AWS calls with the
# ordinary credential chain (`AWS_PROFILE`, SSO, instance role), and the one real
# secret it uses — the cookie sealing key — is named here only by ARN and fetched
# from Secrets Manager at runtime (§8.6 L780). Never put the key itself in here.
#
# The names follow §9.2 L810's `telegator-{env}-{resource}` scheme, so the `dev`
# values are predictable and written out below. The three that are *not* — the
# user pool id, the client id and your account number — have to come from the
# deployed stacks.
#
# The whole file, filled in, is exactly what `infra/lib/app-stack.ts` hands
# Amplify. Once the app is deployed, take the real values from it rather than
# assembling them by hand:
#
#   aws amplify list-apps \
#     --query "apps[?name=='telegator-dev-dashboard'].appId" --output text
#   aws amplify get-app --app-id <id> \
#     --query 'app.environmentVariables' --output json
#
# Running against `dev` also needs credentials for that account, and read access
# to its DynamoDB tables, SQS queues, CloudWatch metrics and log groups, Cognito
# user pool and the session-key secret. Without them the pages compile and render
# but every data read fails — an `AccessDeniedException`, not a crash.

# ── The AWS account ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Not declared by the stack: Amplify and Lambda both set it from the runtime, so
# `handlers/env.ts` deliberately leaves it out. Locally there is no runtime to
# set it, which makes this the one variable you must supply that production does
# not. Every SDK client in `actions/context.ts` is built with it.
AWS_REGION=eu-central-1

# ── Data (§7.2) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# `infra/lib/data-stack.ts` — `config.name("sources")` / `("messages")`.
TELEGATOR_SOURCES_TABLE=telegator-dev-sources
TELEGATOR_MESSAGES_TABLE=telegator-dev-messages

# ── Queues (§7.3) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Full URLs, not names. `123456789012` is your account number — the queue stack
# does not choose it, so this is the one line here you cannot copy verbatim.
# Aggregate and publish are FIFO and carry the suffix AWS requires; analyze is
# Standard and must not.
TELEGATOR_ANALYZE_QUEUE_URL=https://sqs.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/123456789012/telegator-dev-analyze
TELEGATOR_AGGREGATE_QUEUE_URL=https://sqs.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/123456789012/telegator-dev-aggregate.fifo
TELEGATOR_PUBLISH_QUEUE_URL=https://sqs.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/123456789012/telegator-dev-publish.fifo

# §7.3 L610 — "Each has a matching DLQ". The Queues page reads their depths and
# inspects their bodies without consuming them (§8.2 L723).
TELEGATOR_ANALYZE_DLQ_URL=https://sqs.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/123456789012/telegator-dev-analyze-dlq
TELEGATOR_AGGREGATE_DLQ_URL=https://sqs.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/123456789012/telegator-dev-aggregate-dlq.fifo
TELEGATOR_PUBLISH_DLQ_URL=https://sqs.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/123456789012/telegator-dev-publish-dlq.fifo

# ── Manual triggers (§8.4 L752/L754) ─────────────────────────────────────────
# Function *names*. "Scrape now" and "Replay DLQ" invoke the deployed artefact
# rather than running the dashboard's own copy of a stage — which is the whole
# point of the §8.2 L734 boundary, and why these are names and not imports.
TELEGATOR_SCRAPE_FUNCTION_NAME=telegator-dev-scrape
TELEGATOR_DLQ_REPLAY_FUNCTION_NAME=telegator-dev-dlq-replay

# ── Auth (§8.6 L780) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Both are assigned by Cognito at deploy time and cannot be derived from a name.
# The pool id is in the console under the pool `telegator-dev-users`, or:
#   aws cognito-idp list-user-pools --max-results 60
TELEGATOR_USER_POOL_ID=eu-central-1_XXXXXXXXX
TELEGATOR_USER_POOL_CLIENT_ID=xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx

# An absolute origin, scheme included — `lib/auth/routes.ts` passes it as the
# base of `new URL("/oauth2/authorize", …)`, which throws on a bare hostname.
# This is `UserPoolDomain.baseUrl()`, whose prefix is the pool name.
TELEGATOR_COGNITO_DOMAIN=https://telegator-dev-users.auth.eu-central-1.amazoncognito.com

# Where Cognito sends the browser back after sign-in. `infra/lib/auth-stack.ts`
# already registers this exact origin as a dev callback URL, so local sign-in
# works against the deployed pool with no stack change.
TELEGATOR_APP_URL=http://localhost:3000

# The ARN, never the key. The secret holds 32 base64-encoded bytes and seals the
# session cookie: anyone holding it can forge an admin session, so it stays in
# Secrets Manager and is read at request time (`lib/auth/sessionKey.ts`).
TELEGATOR_SESSION_SECRET_ARN=arn:aws:secretsmanager:eu-central-1:123456789012:secret:telegator-dev-session-key

# ── Observability (§8.5 L771) ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The log group the Dashboard's category chart runs Logs Insights over.
TELEGATOR_ANALYZE_LOG_GROUP=/aws/lambda/telegator-dev-analyze

# ── Not needed here ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# TELEGATOR_TELEGRAM_SECRET_ARN is in `handlers/env.ts` but is read only by the
# publish Lambda. The dashboard never posts to Telegram — it re-enqueues onto the
# publish queue — so setting it locally would grant nothing and prove nothing.
